# base/tiers/steps -- TIER 4: the AND and NOT cases of Compiler B's induction.
#
# §8 tier 4. Compiler B compiles a formula phi to a class by induction:
#
#   atom     B1 + Expansion (tier 3)      and      step_and (B2, here)
#   exists   B4 directly                  not      step_not (B3 GUARDED, here)
#
# The atom and exists cases need no lemma of their own -- they are B1/B4 applied
# directly -- so this unit is exactly the two cases that DO. Depends on
# base/axioms (B2, B3, elem_first_subst) and base/tiers/pairs (pair_ex,
# pair_set, pair_uniq).
#
# Copied VERBATIM from the `compiler_b` probe, which the checker accepts.
# It declares no axiom of its own.
#
# WHY BOTH PROOFS RECONCILE PAIR WITNESSES, AND WHY THE GENERATOR MUST NOT:
# pmem(x,y,.) unfolds to `exists p. (kpair(x,y,p) & p in .)`, so a step that
# COMBINES two pmem facts receives two different witnesses and must identify
# them via pair_uniq + elem_first_subst. §8's design note: that reconciliation
# is an artifact of proving each step standalone. A compiler that materialises
# ONE canonical witness per tuple at the top of the chain and threads it never
# reconciles at all. These lemmas are still the right base content -- the
# threading happens in the chain that USES them, not inside them.

claim step_and: forall A. forall B. (exists C. (forall x. (forall y. (set(x) -> (set(y) -> (pmem(x,y,C) <-> (pmem(x,y,A) & pmem(x,y,B))))))))
claim step_not: forall A. (exists C. (forall x. (forall y. (set(x) -> (set(y) -> (pmem(x,y,C) <-> (~ pmem(x,y,A))))))))
